 Nature, the univocity of divine attributes, and the view that God is the sole, immanent cause of all things, with the cause of itself and no reliance on final causes or external will. Finite things refer back to God, and there is only one real sense of causality across the order of being.\n\nSPINOZA’s ontology is complemented by a precise epistemology centered on ideas: the mind is the idea of the body, and there is a fundamental parallelism (isomorphy, isonomie, isology) between mental and bodily processes, such that knowledge of effects implies knowledge of causes. He distinguishes adequate from inadequate ideas and outlines how cognition progresses from affections to clearer concepts, with the notion of common notions (notions communes) playing a crucial role in forming general ideas. He also emphasizes the genetics of understanding; definitions can be nominal or genetic, and the pursuit of intuitive knowledge represents a third kind of understanding beyond imagination and reasoning from experience.\n\nEthically, SPINOZA introduces conatus—the innate drive to persevere in existence—and analyzes how affections (joy, sadness, love, hatred) modulate the power to act. Joy tends to increase power and unite the self with what is loved, while sadness diminishes it. He maintains that freedom is not a property of the will, which is always determined by causes, and he ties human emplace within a deterministic order to the unity of God and Nature. The eternity of essences (species aeternitatis) and the persistence of essence are essential elements, linking ethical life to rational understanding of the divine order.\n\nIn political theory, SPINOZA treats the state as arising from a social contract in which individuals relinquish some power for the collective strength formed by shared affections of hope and fear. This absolutum imperium can take monarchic, aristocratic, or democratic forms, with democracy most closely resembling the ideal of substituting the love of liberty for passion-driven fear and hope.\n\nSPINOZA’s methodological stance is geometric in spirit, emphasizing clear definitions and demonstrations, the role of signs and interpretation in knowledge, and a critique of anthropomorphism. Across these threads, he presents a comprehensive, integrated vision where mind and body, God and nature, essence and existence, and ethics and politics interlink in a single rationalist framework.", "LITTLE HANS:A child mentioned in the text; used in a discussion about developmental and machinated notions of organs and bodily functions, illustrating how questions about why things are as they are arise in children", "VON UEXKULL:Jakob von Uexküll, a theorist cited in the text for defining animal worlds by examining active and passive affects within individuated assemblages", "VIRGINIA WOOLF:Virginia Woolf is an English writer and essayist whose work is repeatedly cited in discussions of women’s writing and gender in literature. In these debates, she is referenced as having been questioned about writing “as a woman,” used to illustrate ideas about becoming-woman and gender dynamics within literary discourse. She is also invoked for the phrase “Saturate every atom,” cited to illustrate arguments about the architecture or construction of a literary work. Additionally, Virginia Woolf, author of Orlando, is linked to discussions of blocks of ages and memory as they relate to Becomings, highlighting how her writing engages time and consciousness. Collectively, these references position Virginia Woolf as a central figure in analyses of gender, form, memory, and temporality within modernist literature.", "LAWRENCE:LAWRENCE is described as a male author cited within a scholarly discourse on gender and becoming-woman. Alongside Miller, LAWRENCE is said to continually tap into and emit “particles” related to women’s proximity to indiscernibility, signaling a preoccupation with the elusive, often undecidable nature of feminine identity. In a related st